---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/use-cases/product-launch';
const title = "Product launch email: keep access promises";
const desc = "Coordinate launch audiences, access windows, variant availability and follow-up. The practical question is whether the email promise still matches the product and checkout when a customer acts.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Must the waitlist always go before VIPs?",
    "answer": "Honor the priority described at signup. Decide and communicate the order before launch; neither waitlist nor VIP status has a universal claim to first access."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can email guarantee that a variant is still available?",
    "answer": "No. Availability can change after rendering or sending. Keep the destination current, define sold-out handling and avoid promising a reservation unless one exists."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should every launch use SMS?",
    "answer": "Only when the opted-in audience, customer purpose and additional cost justify it. Coordinate channels and stop redundant purchase reminders after conversion."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16 md:py-24">
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases">← All use cases</a>
    <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Workflow guide</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-16">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2>Write an access contract</h2>
      <p>Define the product or collection, eligible markets, launch instant and time zone. List any VIP and waitlist priority promised at signup. Early access can mean an earlier shopping window; it does not automatically reserve stock.</p>
      <p>Agree the meaning with merchandising and support before writing creative. If reservations exist, document their quantity and expiry. A launch without priority promises can use a simpler announcement; it does not need artificial tiers or a mandatory teaser series.</p>
      <h2>Capture interest for a specific launch</h2>
      <p>Store launch ID, signup time, source, product or variant interest and communication preference. Avoid overwriting a prior launch interest with a single generic waitlist flag. Explain whether signup means updates, priority access or a stock reservation.</p>
      <p>A collaboration form needs a clear sender and data destination. Do not assume the partner list is available for your own campaigns. Closed or discontinued products need an honest status update rather than an indefinitely open waitlist.</p>
      <h2>Allocate stock and production capacity</h2>
      <p>Review available-to-sell quantities, variant mix, market restrictions and production constraints with operations. Decide what each audience can access and what happens when a variant runs out. Print-on-demand launches need capacity and lead-time limits even without a shelf-stock count.</p>
      <p>Email audience size is not guaranteed demand. Use scenario estimates for expected traffic and orders, but label assumptions. Agree who can pause later waves and change landing-page messaging when allocation or fulfillment capacity changes.</p>
      <h2>Protect early access at the destination</h2>
      <p>An early email link alone may be shareable. If access must be restricted, demonstrate the intended checkout or storefront gate and what happens for a forwarded link or an ineligible visitor. Do not describe an unprotected URL as exclusive inventory access.</p>
      <p>Set send times using an explicit zone and review daylight-saving behavior. Check receipt ordering on controlled accounts. A queued public send should be pausable if the promised priority wave fails; automated send-time optimization may conflict with a fixed access window.</p>
      <h2>Plan creative around availability changes</h2>
      <p>Give teaser, access, public and reminder messages distinct purposes when those stages are useful. Explain product fit, variants, price and fulfillment terms. Only use last-chance language for a real deadline or genuinely limited availability.</p>
      <p>An inventory value rendered at send time can become stale before open or checkout. Use the destination as the current availability reference and define sold-out alternatives. Do not promise that a dynamic block prevents every unavailable-product click.</p>
      <h2>Coordinate channels and neighboring flows</h2>
      <p>Map launch messages against welcome, cart, browse, restock and winback paths. Pause or revise only conflicting asks; unrelated service updates still matter. A recent purchaser of an adjacent product may still want a complementary launch, so suppression should reflect the offer.</p>
      <p>If using SMS or push, confirm the audience permission and incremental purpose of that touch. An email non-open alone is a weak signal of nonreceipt. Define shared message priority and purchaser exit behavior before adding another channel.</p>
      <h2>Handle collaborators and public timing</h2>
      <p>Align partner creative approvals, social posts, landing-page publication and support staffing to the same access contract. Record the launch instant with a named owner for changes. Keep partner-specific codes and terms consistent across destinations.</p>
      <p>A delayed delivery or product issue needs a coordinated update, not an email schedule continuing as planned. Check mobile checkout, market pricing and shipping eligibility for the actual advertised variants before expanding the audience.</p>
      <h2>Plan for buyers and people who missed out</h2>
      <p>Exit purchasers from redundant buy-now reminders for the same item while preserving relevant order help. Route nonbuyers of sold-out variants to an honest restock interest page if another supply is expected. Offer alternatives as alternatives, not silent substitutions.</p>
      <p>Keep launch and back-in-stock interest distinct so an older alert cannot imply access to a new product. If there will be no restock, close the loop. Do not automatically discount immediately after a full-price early access promise.</p>
      <h2>Measure allocation and economics together</h2>
      <p>Report waitlist, VIP and public audiences with explicit overlap rules. Track delivery failures, checkout availability, orders, cancellations, refunds and complaints alongside sell-through. Fast sell-through can reflect constrained supply rather than effective email.</p>
      <p>Compare net contribution after product and operating costs, including any channel fees and incentives. Revenue assigned to a launch message is not the same as incremental revenue. A controlled timing experiment must respect the access already promised; do not withhold a guaranteed benefit merely to create a holdout.</p>
      <h2>Demonstrate, release and debrief</h2>
      <p>Ask an app to demonstrate a variant selling out between waves, an access failure, a purchaser exiting a queued reminder and a launch cancellation. Record who can pause the queue and how to inspect the exact audience. These are proposed exercises, not observed supplier results.</p>
      <p>Budget capture, integration, content changes and peak sends along with license cost. Rehearse the destination and messages on controlled accounts. After the launch, document promise failures and actual stock outcomes, then revise the next launch rather than adopting a fixed four-message or ninety-day recipe.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <nav class="container pb-12 flex flex-wrap gap-4" aria-label="Related workflows">
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases/back-in-stock">Back in stock →</a>
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases/vip-segments">VIP segments →</a>
    <a class="link" href="/use-cases/discount-strategy">Discount strategy →</a>
  </nav>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
